# UNLOCK — Security

This rule contains compact universal security guardrails for Cursor.

Detailed authentication behavior belongs to the canonical auth/security sources.

Do not turn this file into a second security specification.

---

## 1. Trusted Identity

Do not trust authoritative user identity from:

* request bodies;
* query parameters;
* custom headers;
* browser state;
* client-controlled metadata.

Authenticated identity must be established at the trusted server boundary.

Authentication answers who the caller is.

Authorization still determines what that caller may do.

---

## 2. Authorization

Do not assume an authenticated user may access or mutate any requested resource.

Where relevant, enforce accepted:

* ownership;
* CourseMembership;
* role;
* learner;
* instructor;
* Course-state;

rules before protected behavior occurs.

Fail closed when authorization cannot be established safely.

Do not reveal private resource existence unnecessarily.

---

## 3. Secrets

Never commit or expose secrets.

Keep server-only:

* `DATABASE_URL`;
* service-role keys;
* provider credentials;
* tokens;
* private environment values.

Never expose privileged Supabase credentials to the browser.

Do not move secrets into `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`.

Use environment variables and sanitized example configuration where needed.

---

## 4. Privileged Credentials

Privileged/service-role access must not become a shortcut around application authorization.

Use privileged credentials only for an explicit trusted server operation that genuinely requires them.

Do not use them to bypass:

* learner ownership;
* CourseMembership checks;
* instructor/owner authorization;
* accepted product policy.

---

## 5. RLS Baseline

Do not automatically introduce or broaden Row Level Security policies.

Current V1 security includes trusted server-side authentication, application authorization, ownership checks, and existing database controls.

Preserve existing RLS behavior where already present.

New RLS policy design requires explicit security/database scope or an accepted decision.

Never create permissive placeholder policies merely to make access work.

---

## 6. Private Learner Data

Protect learner-specific state such as:

* Attempts;
* UserQuestionProgress;
* DailyPlans;
* private Course access;
* uploaded Course/material content where scoped.

Do not share private learner or Course-scoped data across unrelated users unless explicitly allowed by accepted product behavior.

---

## 7. Data Ownership and Provenance

Respect:

* ownership;
* source;
* version history;
* immutable evidence;
* authorization boundaries.

Do not rewrite or delete data simply because it appears unused.

Destructive data/schema changes require explicit scope and appropriate review.

---

## 8. Client / Server Boundary

Do not expose server-only implementation details through:

* browser bundles;
* API responses;
* logs returned to users;
* client-readable environment values.

Keep security-sensitive decisions on the trusted server side.

UI visibility or hidden buttons are not authorization.

---

## 9. Error Safety

Do not expose:

* raw stack traces;
* SQL;
* database connection details;
* tokens;
* cookies;
* credentials;
* internal authorization logic;

through client-facing failures.

Return controlled errors while retaining useful internal diagnostics safely.

---

## 10. Redirect Safety

Where authentication or API flows accept a redirect target:

* prefer application-local paths;
* reject arbitrary external targets unless explicitly required;
* reject protocol/scheme-relative external redirects;
* validate encoded input before using it.

Do not create open redirects.

---

## 11. Data Minimization

Collect and persist only data with a defined:

* product;
* learning;
* operational;
* security;
* research;

purpose.

Do not add sensitive fields merely because they may be useful later.

---

## 12. Scope Discipline

Do not invent new:

* authorization roles;
* permission models;
* RLS policies;
* tenancy boundaries;
* security infrastructure;

inside unrelated implementation work.

If correct implementation requires a new security decision, surface it explicitly.

---

## 13. Core Principle

> Trust verified server state, not client claims.

> Authentication is not authorization.

> Keep privileged data and credentials server-only.

> Fail closed.

> Do not introduce new security policy accidentally.
